charcoal primary surfaces with lime accents — this is a classic fitness app look. Actually let me think: light theme with charcoal text and lime accents, or dark? The original \"Fitness App\" kit (this resembles a known Dribbble UI kit) uses light background with lime accents. I'll go with a light theme: background #F7F8FA-ish, cards white, charcoal text, lime accents for CTAs and active states. Dark charcoal used for header elements / some cards.\n\nLet me decide the design system:\n- Background: #F6F7F9 (light gray)\n- Surface/cards: #FFFFFF\n- Text primary: #192126\n- Text secondary: #8B9096 / #9BA1A8\n- Brand lime: #BBF246 (buttons, active states, progress)\n- Charcoal: #192126 (headers, dark cards)\n- Level tags: Beginner = lime tinted, Intermediate = orange/amber tinted, Expert = red/pink tinted\n- Analytics tiles: each with own pastel color (heart rate = red/pink, steps = purple/violet, sleep = indigo/blue, water = cyan/blue, calories = lime, training time = orange, cycling = map)\n\nRadii: 16 cards, 24 buttons (pill), small chips 8-12.\nSpacing: 20/24 page padding.\n\n**Components to build** (as reusable components in workspace):\n- Button (reuse given default 1:28? The instructions say \"Reuse the components in 02 · Components\" — the given Button and Text Field and shell). The given components are in the same file, so I can create instances directly. Let me check what the Button component looks like — 350×56. I'll instance it for \"Get Started\" and \"Let's Workout\". Need to check its internal text to see if I can override.\n- Text Field for Search on Home (1:31, 350×73 — hmm 73 tall suggests label + field; maybe just field. I need to inspect).\n- Status Bar (1:38) and Home Indicator (1:49) on every frame.\n- Photos: instance the photo components from 04 · Media (5:13, 5:2, 5:4, 5:15, 5:5, 5:6, 5:9, 5:7, 5:8, 5:10, 5:11, 5:12, 5:17, 5:16, 5:14), Map 5:3, Icons 5:18-5:32.\n\nFor icons, I need to recolor them. Instances allow overriding fills of vector children if they're exposed... Actually in Figma you can override fills on instance children. I'll need to set fills on the vector children of icon instances.\n\nPlan:\n1. First inspect the Button, Text Field, Status Bar, Home Indicator components via use_figma (read-only query) to understand structure.\n2. Check photos' sizes.\n3. Build the 5 screens in 03 · Workspace with auto-layout where helpful.\n4. Create local reusable components in the workspace: Tab Bar (with variants per current tab... actually components with variants; simpler: create a Tab Bar component and instances with icon color overrides; or create TabBar component + Tab item components with active/inactive variants). Brief says \"Design all other states, the headers, the navigation and every business component as reusable components in your file.\" So I should create components: TabItem (active/inactive variants), TabBar, WorkoutCard (popular), PlanRow (with progress), LevelTag (Beginner/Intermediate/Expert), StatChip, RoundRow, DayCell (current/default), ReportTile(s), Banner, Header. Plus Button states maybe.\n5. Wire prototype interactions.\n\nLet me structure the build in manageable sections with use_figma calls:\n\nStep A: Read component internals (Button text, Text Field, Status Bar, Home Indicator) + photo sizes.\n\nStep B: Setup — create a components section in workspace page at e.g. x=0, screens at x offset. Create styles maybe (paint styles / text styles) — optional but nice. I'll create local variables? Keep it simpler: consistent values in code.\n\nStep C: Build components:\n- Icon wrapper approach: icons from Media are components already; I can instance them and recolor. For tab bar active/inactive states, instance the icon and set fill color.\n- Build components: \"Tab/Item\" with variants? To keep manageable, I'll create component sets:\n  - `UI/Tab Item` (variants: Active, Inactive) — but icon differs per tab. Alternative: `Tab Item` component with icon instance swap. Instances support 